TEMPORARY diagnostic-only patch, not a fix. Extremely verbose `logger.debug()`
instrumentation across the KV-offload connector's store/lookup/eviction
paths, added 2026-08-22 to investigate the residual gap left by
`patches/offload-flush-protect-running-request-blocks.patch`: even after
that fix, live production traffic (two large concurrent Hermes Agent
conversations) still showed ~24% of all prompt-token processing going to
unnecessary full recomputes in a 30-minute window, including at least one
request with *zero* offloading store-job activity for its entire ~61K-
token lifetime -- a different-looking failure mode than the "job created
but raced by eviction" one the prior fix targeted. This patch adds enough
log detail to distinguish, for any future occurrence, between: (a) the
request's own req_status was never created (`on_new_request` never fired
for it), (b) `_build_store_jobs` ran but every group's `storable_chunks`
never advanced past `start_chunk_idx` (nothing new considered "storable"
despite real new content), (c) `prepare_store`/`keys_to_store` came back
empty (primary-tier allocation issue), (d) a block got evicted
(`block_pool.py`'s `_maybe_evict_cached_block`) that had no pending job
registered for it at all (the residual gap itself, made directly visible
instead of inferred), and (e) a prefix-hit lookup scan
(`_maximal_prefix_lookup` / `_sliding_window_lookup`) stopped early and
exactly where/why (MISS vs RETRY-deferred).

All new lines are tagged `request-logging offload-verbose:` for easy
grep. Meant to run only long enough to catch the pattern live, then be
reverted -- this is deliberately much chattier than anything that should
stay in a production log long-term (a per-request-per-group summary line
on every offload-relevant scheduler step, plus one line per eviction of
real cached content). Requires `VLLM_LOGGING_CONFIG_PATH` to route the
`vllm` logger at DEBUG to a file (already the case in this repo's
`single-user/logging-to-file-debug.json`, already wired up in
`micke-start.sh`) -- otherwise these lines are silently dropped and cost
only the (cheap, lazy-formatted) `logger.debug()` call overhead.

Requires a server restart to take effect (this only edits the venv's
installed vLLM source, not a running process).

To analyze once collected: grep `qwen.log` for
`request-logging offload-verbose:`, correlate by request_id and, for the
eviction/realloc lines, by block_id and log timestamp (block_pool.py's
`EVICT block_id=N` lines and offloading/scheduler.py's
`REALLOC .../FLUSH ...` lines are in different files/loggers but share
vLLM's own log timestamp format, so nearby timestamps + matching block_id
identify the same event from both sides).

